HISTORICAL / NON-AUTHORITATIVE — DO NOT APPLY
================================================================================

This patch is a record of a fix that has since been made, differently and
properly, in the Apple client. It is kept because
docs/fork/apple-artifact-verification.md describes the reasoning behind it and
that reasoning is still worth reading. It is NOT a prepared fix, NOT part of the
ABI-provenance patch set, and NOT a description of the pinned tree.

# Why it is obsolete

Its base is `d3e8035` — the 29-line, `#if os(iOS)`-guarded ScreenStateObserver that
docs/fork/apple-screen-state-observer.patch once created. The pinned client no
longer has that file. At 90a0e9ae, `Library/Network/ScreenStateObserver.swift` is a
731-line implementation built on two protocols (DarwinNotifyAPI,
ScreenStatePublishing), and the initial-state fact this patch adds is now a
first-class part of it:

    ScreenStatePublishing.reconcileScreenState(on:)   "LEVEL only: a current-state
    ScreenStatePublishing.reconcileLockState(locked:)  read found ... in this state"
    observe(source:provenance:epoch:)                 reads via notify.getState(token:)

So the behaviour this patch was written to add already ships. There is nothing left
to apply.

# Why it still "applies"

`git apply --check` currently SUCCEEDS against the pinned tree, at a 536-line offset,
because the old hunk's context happens to recur further down the rewritten file. A
clean `--check` here is a false positive and must not be read as "still valid".
Applying it would splice a stale pre-rewrite block into code that already handles
the case, so the explicit warning is the point of this header.

# If the screen-state observer ever needs another fix

Write it against the pinned revision and add it to the ABI-provenance set, or land it
in the Apple repository. Do not resurrect this file.

================================================================================
diff --git a/Library/Network/ScreenStateObserver.swift b/Library/Network/ScreenStateObserver.swift
index d3e8035..e37d734 100644
--- a/Library/Network/ScreenStateObserver.swift
+++ b/Library/Network/ScreenStateObserver.swift
@@ -21,2 +21,37 @@
             }
+            // The tunnel can start while the device is already locked and the display already off,
+            // and registration does not deliver a state that already exists: a Darwin dispatch
+            // registration fires on the NEXT post only, so without this read the core considers the
+            // device active until the next lock, unlock or display transition. It then permits
+            // health checks, URLTests and provider refreshes on a phone in a pocket for the whole of
+            // that period - the wake storm the device axis exists to prevent.
+            //
+            // notify_get_state DOES have the answer at registration time, so the initial facts are
+            // read once here. The read is submitted to the observer's own queue so it cannot
+            // interleave with a handler, and it re-reads the CURRENT state rather than the state at
+            // registration, so a transition that arrives first is published first and this publish
+            // simply repeats it - the governor coalesces both into one measurement.
+            //
+            // The lock fact is published first and the display fact second, which decides the one
+            // state where they disagree: a display that is off on a device that is not locked (no
+            // passcode, or the passcode-grace window) ends up PAUSED, because the display fact is
+            // applied last. That is the conservative direction and it matches the design's own
+            // residual - a display that goes off without the device locking leaves the level paused
+            // until the next unlock - rather than releasing speculation for a screen nobody is
+            // looking at.
+            //
+            // Neither publish can invent a sleep boundary. A lock with no measured sleep starts the
+            // measurement and enters the level, and its later release is the boundary; an unlock with
+            // no sleep on record publishes nothing and lifts a pause that was never entered
+            // (TestAFactWithoutASleepPublishesNothing, box_lifecycle_test.go:347).
+            queue.async {
+                var lockState: UInt64 = 0
+                if notify_get_state(self.lockToken, &lockState) == NOTIFY_STATUS_OK {
+                    commandServer.recordLockState(lockState == 1)
+                }
+                var displayState: UInt64 = 0
+                if notify_get_state(self.displayToken, &displayState) == NOTIFY_STATUS_OK {
+                    commandServer.recordScreenState(displayState == 1)
+                }
+            }
         }
